From 437a7280cf95310d518a2f68087aab61403956ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: goldislead <69987043+goldislead@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 03:46:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [GPU] Use EDRAM layout with a single sample addressing scheme

1x, 2x, and 4x views now have same samples, rotated in 4x4 blocks:

4x: u = ((x & ~1) << 1) | (x & 1) | ((s & 1) << 1)
    v = ((y & ~1) << 1) | (y & 1) | (s & 2)
2x: u = (x & ~2) | (s << 1)
    v = ((y & ~1) << 1) | (y & 1) | (x & 2)

Sample bit 0 is horizontal, bit 1 is vertical, and 2x sample 0 is top.

Titles rendering half-resolution 4x passes on full resolution scene use swizzle shaders that are based on 0, 2, 1, 3 remap, while downscale resolve uses sample grouping.

The old 2x2 subdivision decodes intentionally aliased views into shuffled images. Individually changing componenets would just shuffle mismatched layouts, so we change everything at once:
- XeEdramOffsetBytes now does granular guest pixel remapping for resolves, clears, and host depth storage.
- Resolve loaders fetch per-pixel, because neighbors in multisampled buffer no longer share common stride (fast paths for 1x sources are still available).
- Dump generators convert coordinates back to host pixels and samples.
- Transfers create all six possible pairs of direction (1x/2x/4x) + host depth remapping using canonical coordinates, not special-casing particular pairs.
- In 64bpp, the two halves go to the same sample in two adjacent pixels for any sample count.
- Interlock canonicalizes sample 0 address, offsetting +2 columns for horizontal sample bits and +2 rows for vertical.
- 4x coverage passes through.
